      "writtenOn": "2024-02-07T03:36:37Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Stopping interrupts would be good for preventing the IRQ handler from making simultaneous changes, however, that I think I have taken care of by stopping the timer above.  (If the timer overflowed just before I stopped it, and the interrupt status was latched, then execution can only reach this point in this (non-interrupt) routine after the interrupt handler has completed for the last time.)\n\nThe danger that I am noting is if for instance the CMSIS-DAP task is sitting in `queue_blocking_add()` towards the bottom of `dap_goog_gpio_bitbang()`, as the console task reaches this point (in response to `reinit` command).  As you see, `dap_goog_gpio_bitbang()` will execute `bitbang_head \u003d ptr` just before exiting, which is incompatible with the console task having reset all the pointers here, while execution was paused in the middle of `dap_goog_gpio_bitbang()`.\n\nIf we wanted to protect against this, which could make sense, and could apply to the I2C and JTAG protocols as well, then we need to devise some handshake protocol between the console task and the CMSIS-DAP task, to be used when `reinit` runs.  The console task would probably set a global variable, requesting that the CMSIS-DAP task should abort any operation, and go into a \"parked\" state, in which it rejects any requests.  The console task would then probably invoke `task_wake()` to  wake up the CMSIS-DAP task, then then we would have to modify `queue_blocking_add()` and every other callsite of `task_wait_event()` done by the CMSIS-DAP task, to check this global flag, and if set, then immediately bubble up the request to park, until it reaches the top `cmsis_dap_task()` function, which would set another flag and signal the console process that the CMSIS-DAP task now is \"parked\" and will not process any requests that could update state.  Then this code can run, and then the global flag can be changed back to allow the CMSIS-DAP task to again process requests.\n\nGetting safely out of a blocked call stack in the CMSIS-DAP task is not all, we would also need to empty any queued-up data in `cmsis_dap_tx_queue` or `cmsis_dap_rx_queue`, and even further, we would need to revoke any data that the USB driver has pulled off the TX queue, and put into USB buffer memory, ready to serve a future bulk IN transfer.\n\nAll of the above would guard against some scenarios which I do not believe are very likely, though could happen if a previous opentitantool process had been killed before completing some bitbanging, and then a next invocation doing `transport init`.  With all the above, opentitantool would be guaranteed that HyperDebug has returned to a clean CMSIS-DAP protocol state after re-initialization.  I would prefer to look into this in a separate CL, and probably only if it turns out to actually cause test flakiness.",
      "parentUuid": "3be64128_bb6ba742",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1178,
        "startChar": 21,
        "endLine": 1178,
        "endChar": 68
      },
